The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (ct) {
    help = nfct_help(ct);
    xt_ct_put_helper(help);
    nf_ct_netns_put(par->net, par->family);
    nf_ct_destroy_timeout(ct);
    nf_ct_put(info->ct);
}
Reason:
When a template created by xt_CT with a helper or timeout policy is removed, the above code releases the helper and timeout objects without first flushing pending packets in the nf_queue that still reference them. This leaves dangling pointers to the helper module or timeout policy, causing a use-after-free when those objects are subsequently unloaded or removed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (info->helper[0] || info->timeout[0])
    nf_queue_nf_hook_drop(par->net);
Reason:
Before releasing the helper and timeout objects, this call flushes any enqueued packets that still reference the template’s helper or timeout policy. This prevents use-after-free by ensuring that no pending packets hold references to objects that are about to be destroyed.

